INT. STARLITE ROLLER RINK - AFTER HOURS

A cavern of scuffed maple and dead disco light. A mirror ball turns slowly, throwing coins of silver across the walls. The air smells of popcorn oil, rubber wheels, and thirty years of cheap perfume.

ODALYS FERREIRA, 50s, in a faded satin jacket that says STARLITE STAFF, stands at the center of the floor with a push broom. She has been sweeping the same square of wood for some time.

At the skate counter, FITZ MCALLISTER, 60s, owner, sorts a bin of rental skates by size. He doesn't look up. A mug of coffee steams beside a signed contract.

BASHIR KARIMI, 30s, a suit too good for this building, leans against the DJ booth with a briefcase. JUNIPER FERREIRA, 17, headphones around her neck, sits on the rail with her skates in her lap, pretending to lace them.

BASHIR
Mrs. Ferreira, I don't want to rush anyone. But the buyer needs the keys by Friday.

ODALYS
I heard you the first time.

FITZ
She heard you.

ODALYS
Thirty-one years I've swept this floor.

BASHIR
And the new development will keep a plaque --

ODALYS
A plaque.

JUNIPER
Mom.

ODALYS
Don't "Mom" me. You learned to walk on this floor.

JUNIPER
I learned to fall on this floor.

Fitz puts down a skate. Laughs once, softly. Then louder. Then it turns into something else. He covers his face.

FITZ
I signed it, Odie. Tuesday. I couldn't tell you.

The broom drops. It clatters across the maple, a flat hollow sound that rings up into the rafters.

ODALYS
You what.

FITZ
The roof is gone. The boiler is gone. I've been paying the electric with my pension --

ODALYS
YOU DIDN'T ASK ME.

BASHIR ^
Please, let's all just take a breath --

ODALYS
(to Bashir)
Get out.

JUNIPER
(to Bashir)
Get out.

Bashir picks up his briefcase and, for once, is quick about it. The glass door closes behind him with a polite chime.

The mirror ball keeps turning. Odalys sits down hard on the floor. Fitz comes around the counter and sits beside her, knees creaking. Neither speaks.

Juniper stands. Laces her skates properly this time. She rolls out onto the empty rink, slow, then faster, and loops the whole oval alone.

ODALYS
(watching, softly)
She's got your father's stride.

FITZ
She's got yours.

Odalys wipes her cheek with the heel of her hand. Then stands, offers Fitz a hand up. He takes it.

ODALYS
Put on the Bee Gees.

FITZ
We're closed.

ODALYS
Put on the Bee Gees, Fitzgerald.

He crosses to the booth. Taps a button. A bass line thumps out of tired speakers. The silver light sweeps the three of them as Juniper glides past, arms out, laughing.
